1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,960
This is AI Video Production Editor:

2
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:06,560
a free, open-source film studio for AI video.

3
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:09,280
It takes a project from script to storyboard,

4
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:14,160
from storyboard to generated shots, and from there to the edit and the export.

5
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:17,600
Everything lives in one project folder on your own machine,

6
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:20,000
and you bring your own API keys.

7
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:22,240
In the next few minutes, you'll install it,

8
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:25,540
connect a provider, and set up your first project.

9
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:30,280
The quickest way in is the desktop app for macOS or Windows,

10
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:33,320
on the releases page of the GitHub repository.

11
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:37,240
If you'd rather run it from source, you need Node twenty-two or newer.

12
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:42,520
Clone the repository, run npm install, then npm run electron dev.

13
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:45,820
That starts the dev server and opens the desktop app.

14
00:00:46,567 --> 00:00:50,407
On first launch, a short welcome guide walks you through four steps.

15
00:00:50,487 --> 00:00:52,647
First, how you want to work.

16
00:00:52,727 --> 00:00:56,147
Just getting started gives you a simple, guided studio.

17
00:00:56,167 --> 00:00:58,627
I'm a filmmaker opens the full suite.

18
00:00:58,647 --> 00:01:01,287
Next, pick a dark or a light look.

19
00:01:01,367 --> 00:01:03,027
Then connect AI.

20
00:01:03,047 --> 00:01:07,187
You only need one key to get started, and Google Gemini has a free tier.

21
00:01:07,207 --> 00:01:10,467
Keys are stored on your machine and only go to the provider.

22
00:01:10,487 --> 00:01:13,187
You can also skip this and add keys later.

23
00:01:13,207 --> 00:01:15,507
The last step shows what you can do.

24
00:01:15,527 --> 00:01:19,027
With no key connected yet, the studio then opens Settings,

25
00:01:19,047 --> 00:01:21,267
where all thirteen providers live.

26
00:01:21,287 --> 00:01:23,527
Close it for now, and you're in.

27
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:25,447
Here's the studio.

28
00:01:25,467 --> 00:01:28,647
The sidebar holds every workspace, grouped into Project,

29
00:01:28,667 --> 00:01:31,047
Create, Edit, Review and Deliver.

30
00:01:31,067 --> 00:01:35,547
At the top, Simple, Standard and Pro switch how much of the studio you see.

31
00:01:35,707 --> 00:01:37,847
Simple keeps just the essentials.

32
00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:42,427
Pro shows everything, from Set Design and Scene Map to Color and Analysis.

33
00:01:42,507 --> 00:01:47,147
Activity keeps track of running jobs, and AI Tools opens the assistant.

34
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:51,080
Before you create anything, choose a project folder.

35
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:54,280
Open the project menu and pick Choose Project Folder.

36
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:58,520
The studio writes the project file, a backup and a media folder into it,

37
00:01:58,540 --> 00:02:00,040
and saves as you work.

38
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:03,020
The badge switches from No folder to Saved.

39
00:02:03,767 --> 00:02:06,147
The Project Hub is the heart of the app.

40
00:02:06,167 --> 00:02:07,347
First, pick a format:

41
00:02:07,367 --> 00:02:11,347
a feature, a short film, a microdrama or a commercial.

42
00:02:11,367 --> 00:02:14,147
The seven steps on the left are your production path,

43
00:02:14,167 --> 00:02:16,147
and step one is the script.

44
00:02:16,167 --> 00:02:19,827
We'll paste in Rainline, the demo script from the examples folder,

45
00:02:19,847 --> 00:02:22,307
and give it a title in the story bible.

46
00:02:22,327 --> 00:02:26,547
With a key connected, Analyze Script breaks the story down into characters

47
00:02:26,567 --> 00:02:28,887
and locations for the next steps.

48
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:32,253
From there, each step builds on the last.

49
00:02:32,273 --> 00:02:34,653
World is optional lore and rules.

50
00:02:34,673 --> 00:02:38,893
Concept is where you cast characters and design environments and props.

51
00:02:38,913 --> 00:02:41,800
Storyboard turns scenes into shots and frames,

52
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:44,493
with camera, lens and aspect ratio.

53
00:02:44,513 --> 00:02:47,693
Filming sends approved frames to video models like Veo,

54
00:02:47,713 --> 00:02:49,213
Kling or Seedance.

55
00:02:49,233 --> 00:02:51,666
Review checks continuity across your shots,

56
00:02:51,686 --> 00:02:54,433
and Marketing makes posters and thumbnails.

57
00:02:55,167 --> 00:02:57,147
You don't have to use the full pipeline.

58
00:02:57,167 --> 00:02:59,947
Images and Video are standalone generators.

59
00:02:59,967 --> 00:03:04,267
Each one shows what the selected model still needs before you generate,

60
00:03:04,287 --> 00:03:06,907
and an estimated cost on your own key.

61
00:03:06,927 --> 00:03:10,607
And in Node Space, you can build the same work as a graph.

62
00:03:11,367 --> 00:03:13,987
Your clips land in the project's media.

63
00:03:14,007 --> 00:03:16,227
The Timeline works like an editing suite,

64
00:03:16,247 --> 00:03:18,787
with pages for cut, color and sound.

65
00:03:18,807 --> 00:03:21,907
And Export renders the result, with presets for YouTube,

66
00:03:21,927 --> 00:03:23,767
TikTok and Instagram.

67
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:26,400
That's your first ten minutes.

68
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:29,280
Next, open First Ten Minutes in the docs folder,

69
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:32,080
and run the Rainline demo with a key connected.

70
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:34,560
The project is open source under the GPL,

71
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:37,760
so issues, ideas and pull requests are welcome.

72
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:41,600
And if it helps you make something, a star on GitHub helps other filmmakers

73
00:03:41,620 --> 00:03:42,580
find it.
